## 12. Example end-to-end release workflow

For ShopEasy version `2.4.1`:

1. Start from the correct production or development branch according to the team's strategy.
2. Create `release/2.4.1` if using release branches.
3. Stabilize the release and run tests.
4. Fix production issues in the appropriate branch.
5. Ensure fixes reach future development branches.
6. Set the app version to `2.4.1`.
7. Assign a valid, increasing build number.
8. Build and test the exact candidate binary.
9. Record the commit SHA, app version, build number, and build artifacts.
10. Create and push an annotated Git tag for the exact submitted commit.
11. Submit the build to the app store.
12. If a new submission is required, increment the build number and record the new candidate.
13. Monitor crashes, API errors, and user feedback after release.
14. Use feature flags or a hotfix release when appropriate.
15. Maintain backend compatibility with older supported app versions.

---

## 13. Quick reference

| Situation | Recommended approach |
|---|---|
| Developing an isolated feature | Create a feature branch |
| Stabilizing a specific release | Use a release branch if the workflow calls for one |
| Fixing a bug in a live app | Start from the relevant production tag or release branch |
| Applying one fix to another branch | Consider cherry-pick |
| Integrating related branch changes | Merge |
| Resubmitting a corrected build | Increase the build number as required by the platform |
| Identifying submitted source code | Tag the exact submitted commit and record its SHA |
| Gradually enabling a feature | Use feature flags or remote config |
| Adding API functionality | Prefer backward-compatible changes |
| Making a breaking API change | Plan versioning, migration, and deprecation |

